  "id": "1708__BayOfBengal__Jaillot-Mortier__Andaman-Ceylon-Maldives",
  "year": 1708,
  "approx": false,
  "region": "BayOfBengal",
  "maker": "Mortier",
  "date_label": "1708",
  "title": "The Bay of Bengal, Ceylon, the Maldives and the Andaman Islands",
  "short": "The Bay of Bengal, Ceylon, the Maldives and the Andaman Islands",
  "byline": "Pierre Mortier · 1700, issued 1708",
  "brief": "A large sea chart of the islands and waters around India – Ceylon, the Maldives and the Andamans – first issued by Pierre Mortier at Amsterdam in 1700 and bound again into his 1708 edition of Jaillot’s atlas. A French-titled chart, Dutch-engraved, on claimed Portuguese authority.",
  "image": {
   "src": "img/display/1708__BayOfBengal__Jaillot-Mortier__Andaman-Ceylon-Maldives.jpg",
   "alt": "Carte Particuliere d'une Partie d'Asie ou sont les Isles d'Andemaon, Ceylan, les Maldives",
   "width": 1800,
   "height": 1141,
   "dzi": "img/dzi/1708__BayOfBengal__Jaillot-Mortier__Andaman-Ceylon-Maldives.dzi"
  },
  "thumb": {
   "alt": "The Bay of Bengal, Ceylon, the Maldives and the Andaman Islands",
   "width": 700,
   "height": 444
  },
  "room": 2,
  "deccan": [],
  "prose": "<p class=\"subhead\">A transnational atlas object</p>\n<p>Pierre Mortier first issued this chart in 1700 in his <em>Suite du Neptune François</em>, claiming the charts of Portuguese pilots as his source, and bound it again into his 1708 Amsterdam edition of Jaillot’s <em>Atlas Nouveau</em>. The sheet belongs to no single nation.</p>\n<p class=\"subhead\">The maritime region</p>\n<p>The sheet foregrounds the islands and waters through which traffic moved: Ceylon, the Maldives, the Andamans and the surrounding seas. At this scale, maritime geography becomes a region in its own right.</p>\n<p class=\"subhead\">Authority through enlargement</p>\n<p>Mortier’s large-format atlases turned borrowed source maps into imposing commercial plates. Scale, engraving and presentation amplify authority even where the underlying geography remains derivative. The map is an artefact of publishing competition as much as of geographical observation.</p>\n<p class=\"subhead\">On the sheet</p>\n<p>The sheet is two charts joined. The right-hand cartouche is the one the catalogue names – <em>Carte particulière d’une partie d’Asie où sont les isles d’Andemaon, Ceylan, les Maldives</em> – and the left-hand one is its partner, <em>Partie occidentale d’une partie d’Asie où sont les isles de Zocotora, de l’Amirante</em>, so that together they carry the Indian Ocean from the African coast to Sumatra. Rhumb lines radiate from compass roses on both halves; the coasts are washed in red, yellow and green; the interior of India is filled with the Mughal provinces of the Sanson tradition, <em>Empire du Grand Mogol</em> across the north and Visapour, Golconde, Bisnagar and Decan in the peninsula. The Maldives are a long dotted ribbon annotated as ‘composed of 12,000 islands or rocks’, and <em>Ocean Oriental Indien</em> and <em>Grande Mer des Indes</em> are lettered across the water in capitals larger than any name on land.</p>\n<p class=\"subhead\">The gaze</p>\n<p>Ceylon, the Maldives and the Andamans hold the centre and the subcontinent is pushed to the margin. For a Dutch publisher selling to French buyers on Portuguese information, the valuable India was the one ships had to thread through, and Mortier enlarged it to match its price.</p>",
